## 1. Introduction
Glycyrrhizic acid (GA), a natural triterpene glycoside isolated from licorice root, has been reported to exhibit a range of potentially beneficial biological effects, including anti-inflammatory, anti-allergenic, and anti-cancer activities [1]. GA is also a natural sweetener, with about a 170-fold higher degree of sweetness than sucrose, and is widely used in the food industry for this purpose. GA consists of a hydrophilic triterpenoid aglycon (glucuronic acid) conjugated with hydrophobic disaccharide glycogen fragments (glycyrrhetic acid) [2]. GA has a relatively low oral bioavailability, which has mainly been attributed to its poor solubility in aqueous gastrointestinal fluids [1]. The water-solubility and bioavailability of GA can be enhanced by encapsulating it within colloidal delivery systems, such as liposomes [3], chitosan nanoparticles [4,5], and cyclodextrin (CD) nanocarriers [6].
CDs are cyclic, hollow, truncated cones that are constructed from natural oligosaccharides composed of $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ -1,4-linked glucosyl units [6]. The most common and studied CDs are a-, $\upbeta\mathrm{-}$ , and $\gamma$ - CDs, which have 6, 7, and 8 glucosyl units, respectively. CDs have a hydrophilic surface and a hydrophobic cavity, which is capable of forming noncovalent inclusion complexes with a variety of small guest molecules. Several researchers have explored the interactions between CD and GA. Oda and Kuroda [7] confirmed the formation of 1:1 inclusion complexes of GA with $\upbeta$ -CD and $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ . Izutani et al. [1] reported that the binding affinity of GA to $\gamma$ -CD was higher than that of GA to $\upbeta$ -CD. However, the encapsulation efficiency of GA was still limited due to the size of the hydrophobic cavities within the CD molecules.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted growing interest because of their unique functional characteristics, such as tunable size and shape, high surface-to-volume ratio, and ultra-high porosity [8–12]. CD-based MOFs (CD-MOFs) are assembled from a natural product $\dot{\boldsymbol{\gamma}}$ -CD) and alkali metal salts (potassium ions), which leads to the formation of a body-centered cubic extended structure [13–15]. Due to their good water solubility, excellent biocompatibility, and low toxicity, CD-MOFs have potential applications as delivery or carrier systems for bioactive ingredients or drugs.
The size of CD-MOFs is critical for many of their applications. For instance, their size may impact their ability to interactions with other molecules, cells, and tissues, as well as their ability to be taken up by cells through paracellular penetration, endocytosis, or mucosa-associated lymphoid tissues [16]. As a result, it may be possible to tune their functional properties as bioactive delivery systems by controlling their particle size. Furthermore, nanoscale CD-MOFs have a higher surface area and adsorption capacity that conventional microscale CD-MOFs [17,18]. Several strategies have been adopted for controlling the size of MOFs. CD-MOFs are usually prepared using a vapor diffusion method at ambient temperature, which takes about 7 days [15]. A modified vapor diffusion method was used to prepare CD-MOFs by vapor diffusion at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $6{-}24\mathrm{h}$ by the addition of cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) [19]. However, the particle size of porous CD-MOF crystals could only be controlled within a limited range $(2-10\upmu\mathrm{m})$ using this method. More recently, much effort has been made to create smaller particle sizes using microwave, sonochemical, and mechanochemical methods [20,21]. For instance, CDMOFs with particle sizes ranging from nanometers to micrometers have been synthesized within a relatively short time $(1{-}6\mathrm{h})$ using a pure seed-mediated method [22]. However, a simple, ecofriendly method is urgently needed to shorten the synthesis time, create smaller crystals, and meet the needs of industrialization.
The main objective of this study was to develop a new approach of rapidly fabricating nanoscale CD-MOFs by combining a facile seed-mediated method with sonication. Short-chain starch nanoparticles from debranched starch were used as seeds to allow the $\gamma$ -CD molecules to easily adhere to their surfaces through interactions between the short-chain regions in the starch and the hydrophobic cavities of the CDs. In this way, $\gamma$ -CDs can easily be arranged into highly ordered structures, thereby accelerating nucleation and shortening the synthesis time. Compared to conventional methods, sonication offers a facile, efficient, and environmentally friendly way to produce these nanostructured materials [23]. Several researchers have explored the facile synthesis of MOF porous materials using sonication [24–26]. Khan and Jhung [20] reviewed the applications of sonication to synthesize MOFs. They found that sonication was faster than conventional methods and achieved smaller nanoparticles [20]. However, to the best of our knowledge, this is an first report on the synthesis of nanoscale CD-MOFs that involves combining a seeding approach with a sonication approach. Moreover, no studies have investigated the effects of sonication on the structural, physicochemical, loading, and toxicological properties of the nanoparticles formed.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
$\gamma\cdot$ -CD $.{\geq}98\%$ purity) and Glycyrrhizic acid $98\%$ purity) were purchased from Sigma (Shanghai, China). Waxy maize starch $(\approx99\%$ amylopectin) was obtained from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. (Shanghai, China). All other chemicals were reagent grade.
## 2.2. Synthesis of c-cyclodextrin metal-organic frameworks (c-CDMOFs)
The seeds of short-chain starch nanoparticles (SNP) and $\gamma$ -CDMOF crystals were prepared following the method reported by Qiu et al. [27], with some modifications. A mother solution was prepared by mixing $0.25~\mathrm{mmol}$ $\gamma$ -CD and 2 mmol KOH in $10\mathrm{mL}$ aqueous solution. Then, $10\mathrm{mg}$ of SNP seeds were added and the mixed solution was filtered through a $0.45\upmu\mathrm{m}$ organic filter membrane. A high-intensity sonicator (JYD-900L, Shanghai Zhisun Equipment Co., Ltd, 20 to 25 kHz, 900 W $/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , $50\%$ power) was used for ultrasonic processing of the resulting samples for different times (0, 3, 5, 10, and $15\mathrm{min}$ ). In our preliminary experiments, we found that using too high an ultrasonic frequency (40– $50\mathrm{kHz},$ adversely effected the structural integrity of the crystals formed. For this reason, we selected an ultrasonic frequency of $20{-}25\operatorname{kHz}$ to treat the samples. After sonication, $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of MeOH was slowly added to the solution using a peristaltic pump to trigger precipitation. The MOF crystals formed were collected by centrifugation at $_{10,000\mathrm{g}}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ and washed with absolute MeOH twice; they were then freeze-dried to obtain a powder.
## 2.3. Preparation of glycyrrhizic acid-loaded cyclodextrin metal-organic frameworks
The GA-loaded CD-MOFs were prepared following a method described previously [28], with some modifications. A weighed amount of GA was dispersed in $10\mathrm{mL}$ phosphate buffer solution $\left(\mathsf{p H}\ 7.4\right)$ and then heated at $80^{\circ}C$ to obtain a GA solution. Then, $100\mathrm{mg}$ of $\gamma$ -CD-MOF crystals were added to the GA solution and the mixture was agitated using a magnetic stirrer at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for different times at a constant stirring rate of $200\mathrm{rpm}$ . To determine the encapsulation efficiency, the nanosuspension was precipitated and centrifuged at $_{10,000\mathrm{g}}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ The free GA was collected and quantified spectrophotometrically at $256.5\:\mathrm{nm}$ . The amount of GA bound to $\gamma$ -CD-MOF was then calculated by subtracting the free GA content from the total GA content added to the system.
## 2.4. Particle characterization
The morphology of the CD-MOF crystals was determined using a transmission electron microscope (TEM) operating at an acceleration voltage of $200\mathrm{kV}.$ . The mean crystal diameter and polydispersity index (PDI) of the $\gamma$ -CD-MOFs were measured using dynamic light-scattering (DLS) (Zetasizer Nano ZS, Malvern Instruments Inc., Malvern, UK). DLS measures scattered intensity autocorrelation function, whose decay rate is linked to the diffusion coefficient. The crystalline structures of the samples were analyzed using a Bruker X-ray diffractometer (D2 PHASER, Bruker AXS Inc., Germany) at $40\mathrm{kV}$ and $30\mathrm{mA}$ Cu-Ka radiations [29]. Diffractograms were recorded from $3^{\circ}$ to $40^{\circ}$ (2h) at a scan rate of $2^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}$ . The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area, Langmuir surface area, and pore diameters of the CD-MOFs prepared with different sonication times were measured using a gas absorption system (Micrometrics ASAP 2010, USA). In order to remove the interstitial solvents, the CD-MOFs were immersed in dichloromethane for 3 days and then dried under vacuum at $50^{\circ}\mathsf C$ overnight. The samples were degassed at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ before determination at vacuum (10–5 Torr). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was conducted by means of synchronous thermal analysis (STA449C/4/G, Netzsch, Germany). Fourier transformation infrared (FTIR) spectra were measured using a IR spectrometer (Nicolet IS10, ThermoNicolet Inc., USA) equipped with an attenuated total reflection (ATR) accessory. The cytotoxicity of the CD-MOF was determined using a cell viability tests following a method described previously [30]
## 2.5. Statistical analysis
Each measurement was conducted at least three times and the results were used to calculate the mean $\pm$ standard deviation values. Statistical analysis was carried out using commercial software (SPSS 17.0). Differences were considered at a significance level of $95\%$ $\mathrm{\P}<0.05\$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Morphology and crystal size
The morphology of CD-MOFs prepared using different sonication times were measured (Fig. 1). In the absence of sonication, a large number of $\gamma$ -CD floccules with some separate nanoparticle seeds could be observed in the TEM images (data not shown), indicating that crystalline CD-MOFs were not formed. The formation of MOF crystals mainly went through two stages: nucleation and growth. When SNP seeds were present, the $\gamma$ -CD molecules could easily attach to their surfaces and self-assemble, thereby promoting nucleation. After $3\mathrm{min}$ sonication, cube-shaped crystals with diameters below $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ were formed. Interestingly, some small cubes could be seen around the large MOF crystals. This phenomenon has not been reported before and may provide some insight into the growth mechanism of the crystals. Crystallization is a dynamic and balanced process involving the formation of small crystal nuclei and the growth of larger crystals. After 5 min sonication, the crystalline $\gamma$ -CD-MOFs became smaller and denser (lower electron transmittance, Fig. 1B). After $10\mathrm{min}$ sonication, even smaller and denser crystals were obtained (Fig. 1C). The mean diameter of the CD-MOFs decreased significantly with increasing sonication time (Table 1): $894\pm48~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (3 min); $316\pm23~\mathrm{{nm}}$ $(5\mathrm{{min})}$ ; and, $234\pm19\mathrm{nm}$ $10\mathrm{min}^{\cdot}$ ). Moreover, the polydispersity index also decreased with increasing sonication time (0.252 to 0.159), indicating that the CD-MOFs formed had a narrow uniform particle size distribution. These results clearly show that sonication led to the formation of smaller particles, which may have been due to its ability to inhibit crystal aggregation. Furthermore, sonication may have promoted nucleation and prevented the crystals from growing too large. Several other researchers have also reported that sonication can reduce synthesis time [31], enhance product yield [32], and improve adsorption performances [33,34]. In our study, when the sonication time was further increased to $15\mathrm{min}$ destruction of the crystalline $\gamma$ -CD-MOFs occurred (Fig. 1D), which may have been due to damage to the crystalline structure caused by excessive mechanical stresses or heating caused by prolonged sonication.
## 3.2. X-ray diffraction analysis
The impact of sonication time on the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the CD-MOFs was determined (Fig. 2). In the absence of sonication, broad weak peaks were observed at 4.0, 5.7, 7.0, 13.6, and $16.5^{\circ}$ , indicating that the crystalline structure of the CD-MOFs synthesized using this condition were imperfect. Similar results have also been reported previously [19,22,35]. The crystallinity of the CD-MOFs was clearly improved by sonication (Fig. 2). After $10\mathrm{min}$ sonication, the intensity of the peaks in the XRD patterns increased significantly, especially at 4.0, 5.7, and $7.0^{\circ}$ , indicating that more perfect crystals were formed. The utilization of sonication therefore offered an efficient way of obtaining nanostructured materials.
## 3.3. Surface analysis
The impact of sonication time on the surface properties of the cCD-MOF crystals is summarized in Table 2. The measured pore diameter of CD-MOF crystals was in the range $2.41-3.45~\mathrm{nm}$ , indicating a typical characteristic of mesoporous materials. The CDMOF-10 had a BET (Langmuir) surface area of 926.6 (1141.5) $\mathbf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , which was much higher than the CD-MOF-3 and CDMOF-5, indicating that sonication was an effective means of improving the crystal structure. However, for the CD-MOF-15, there was a slight decrease in surface area (Table 2), which can be attributed to the fact that prolonged sonication promoted crystal disruption and aggregation (Fig. 1D). The BET (Langmuir) surface area results were significantly higher than previously reported [17,35], although lower than the theoretical BET surface areas $(1030\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}),$ [35] and other reported MOFs based on nonfood grade organic linkers and transition metals [36,37]. Lv et al. [17] reported that the Langmuir surface area of micron-sized CD-MOF and nanoscale CD-MOF were 786 and $668\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Liu et al. [35] reported that the BET surface area of CD-MOF crystals was $495\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ when the preparation period was shortened from $26\mathrm{h}$ to $^{6\mathrm{h}}$ .
## 3.4. Loading kinetics
The impact of sonication time on the loading kinetics of GA to $\gamma$ -CD-MOF is shown in Fig. 3. The amount of GA loaded into the $\gamma$ -CD-MOFs increased significantly with increasing sonication time. GA loading was rapid during the first $30\mathrm{min}$ indicating that the binding site of $\gamma$ -CD-MOF could easily be accessed by the GA molecules. After about $40\mathrm{min}$ , there was no further increase in the amount of GA loaded into the $\gamma$ -CD-MOFs, suggesting that they had become saturated. Similar findings have also been reported by Liu et al. [38], who found that the interactions between polyphenols and starch nanoparticles occurred mainly within the first $30\mathrm{min}$ , at which point the adsorption reached equilibrium.
Sonication time clearly had a pronounced impact on the amount of GA that could be incorporated into the $\gamma$ -CD-MOFs (Fig. 3). The amount of GA bound to the $\gamma$ -CD-MOFs increased significantly with increasing sonication time. A maximum loading capacity of around $850~\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mg}$ was obtained at a sonication time of $10\mathrm{min}$ . The increase in loading capacity with sonication (up to $10\mathrm{min}$ ) can mainly be attributed to the decrease in $\gamma$ -CD-MOF size, and therefore increase in available surface area. However, prolonged sonication $\cdot15\mathrm{min}\rangle$ led to crystal disintegration and aggregation (Fig. 1D), thereby reducing the surface area available to bind to the GA.
## 3.5. Loading isotherms
Loading isotherms were determined at 30, 40, 50, and $60^{\circ}C$ by measuring the amount of GA bound to the $\gamma$ -CD-MOFs as the total amount of GA in the system was increased (Fig. 4). As expected, the amount of GA bound to the $\gamma$ -CD-MOFs increased as the total GA concentration increased but then reached a constant value, which suggests that the binding sites had become saturated. Other researchers have reported similar results for polyphenols interacting with amylopectin nanoparticles [39].
The amount of GA bound to the $\gamma$ -CD-MOFs at saturation increased with increasing temperature (Fig. 4). For instance, at the highest GA level used, the amount of GA bound to the $\gamma$ -CDMOFs was around 287 and $1027\:\upmu\mathrm{g/mg}$ at 30 and $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , respectively. These results suggest that the affinity of the GA for the binding sites in the $\gamma$ -CD-MOFs increased with temperature. This effect may have been partly because of the increase in watersolubility of the GA with temperature, as well as an increase in the strength of any hydrophobic interactions. When the temperature was further increased, the binding became weaker because of the increase in entropy effects with temperature. In comparison, Bernela et al. [3] reported that the encapsulation efficiency of GA in chitosankatira gum nanoparticles was around $85\%$ (about $424\upmu\mathrm{g/mg})$ .
To establish the possible maximum loading capacity of GA to the CD-MOFs, the Langmuir isotherm model was used:
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\mathsf{Q}_{\mathrm{e}}=\mathsf{Q}_{\mathrm{m}}\mathsf{K}\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{e}}/(1+\mathsf{K}\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{e}}),}\end{array}
$$
Here $\mathsf{Q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ is the amount of loaded GA $(\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mg})$ , $\mathrm{\sf{Q}}_{\mathrm{{m}}}$ is the maximum apparent loading capacity $(\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{mg})$ , K is the binding affinity constant; and $C_{\mathrm{e}}$ is the non-adsorbed GA concentration at equilibrium (mM).
The $\mathrm{\sf{Q}}_{\mathrm{{m}}}$ , K, and R values obtained from the Langmuir isotherm equation are presented in Table 3. The high correlation coefficient $\left(\mathbb{R}^{2}>0.98\right)$ suggests that there is a good fit between the experimental data and the Langmuir binding isotherm equation. The binding capacity of GA to the CD-MOFs was predicted to be relatively high $\mathrm{^{\prime}Q_{m}=2594\upmu g/m g}\mathrm{)}$ when the binding temperature was $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ suggesting that CD-MOF crystals could be used as an effective nanocarrier for GA.
## 3.6. Fourier transformation infrared spectroscopy
The FTIR spectra of GA, $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ , and GA-loaded CD-MOFs are shown in Fig. 5. The strong absorption peak observed at 3680 to $3000\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was attributed to the $0{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ stretching vibration of the GA, $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ , and CD-MOFs. Infrared (IR) spectra of the GA showed absorption bands at 2932 and $2865\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ $1718\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponding to the carboxyl group, and 1650– $1600{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ corresponding to the carbonyl group. Comparing the IR spectrum of the CD-MOF crystals to that of the GA-loaded CDMOFs, the absorption peak at $1718\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (carboxyl group) disappeared, while absorption peaks at $2865~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (CAH) and $1600{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ (carbonyl group) appeared, indicating the presence of interactions between GA and CD-MOFs. Similarly, Bernela et al.
[3] reported that the absorption peak of GA-loaded chitosankatira gum nanoparticles at $1718\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ disappeared due to electrostatic interactions between amino groups of chitosan and carboxyl groups of GA.
## 3.7. Thermogravimetric analysis
Thermogravimetric analysis was used to provide information about the composition of the samples. As shown in Fig. 6, a mass loss of $7-10\%$ was observed at $20{-}100^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ which could be due to the dehydration of free water in GA and GA-loaded CD-MOFs. The main mass loss of GA occurred in the temperature range from 219 to $403^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (Fig. 6A), and the maximum thermal degradation temperature was approximately $351^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (Fig. 6B). For the GAloaded CD-MOFs, the main mass loss occurred from 255 to $373^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The maximum degradation temperature increased with increasing sonication time (Fig. 6B): $306.6^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (GA-MOF-0); $309.4^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (GA-MOF-3); $311.8^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (GA-MOF-5); and $314.2^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (GAMOF-10). This increase in thermal stability could have been due to the improvement of the crystalline structure with sonication time, which is consistent with the XRD results.
## 3.8. Cytotoxicity
It is important to establish the potential toxicity of any newly developed delivery systems. For this reason, the cell viability of GA-loaded CD-MOFs was measured (Fig. 7). CD-MOFs synthesized using different sonication times all showed a high cell viability $(>90\%)$ when present at low concentrations $(<500~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ . Even at a high concentration $(1000\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ , the cell viability was still above $85\%$ , indicating that the GA-loaded CD-MOFs were safe and nontoxic. Optical microscopy images also supported the fact that the GA-CD-MOFs exhibited low toxicity in this cell model. The Caco-2 cells showed adherent growth ability (Fig. 8), indicating that they survived and grew well. No clear difference could be seen in the morphology of Caco-2 cells between cells incubated with
CD-MOFs and control cells. Similarly, Abuçafy et al. [40] reported that $\gamma$ -CD-MOFs exhibited little toxicity in Caco-2 cells.
## 4. Conclusion
We have developed an innovative nanoparticle fabrication method that improves upon the methods described previously [15,19,22]. Our method involves combining a seed-mediated nucleation method with sonication to form $\gamma$ -CD-MOFs. The facile, efficient, and environmentally friendly method developed here shortens the synthesis time from a few hours to a few minutes and creates smaller and more uniform crystals. These advantages are mainly because the seeds act as nucleation sites for the $\gamma$ -CDs and sonication facilitates the formation of small uniform crystals by promoting effective mixing and inhibiting particle aggregation. The mean particle diameter of CD-MOFs decreased significantly with increasing sonication time: from $894\mathrm{nm}$ at $3\mathrm{min}$ to $234\mathrm{nm}$ at $10\mathrm{min}$ . CD-MOFs synthesized using the seedmediated-sonication method also had higher crystallinity and better thermal stability than those prepared without. The binding capacity of the CD-MOFs for GA increased with sonication time (from 3 to $10\mathrm{min}$ ), with around $1027\upmu\mathrm{g/mg}$ being bound after $10\mathrm{min}$ sonication. This amount was appreciably higher than that reported for chitosan-katira gum nanoparticles (around $424\upmu\mathrm{g/mg})$ [3]. The cell cytotoxicity results confirmed that the GA-loaded CD-MOFs appeared to be safe and nontoxic. In summary, the CD-MOFs fabricated in this study have potential applications as nanocarriers in the pharmaceutical, supplement, and food fields.
